obs and  in order for the entire city to be one  cohesive functioning unity which is  better for everybody within the city so  as opposed to like courage which doesn't  need to be possessed by the ordinary  person on the street the craftsperson as  Plato will call him all parts of the  city the the Guardians the the ordinary  people the craftspeople and the soldiers  or the lesser Guardians auxiliaries all  of them need to be temperate all of them  need to be so thrown in Greek and he  talks about this as being a kind of  harmony or Concord between the parts  there are some some musical analogies  going on that are very clearly intended  by Plato he also uses the term order or  orderliness and the Greek term that he  uses there that we translate is cosmos  which is quite interesting because you  know we think of all cosmos that's the  universe right for the Greeks cosmos is  universe insofar as it's not just a  crazy mess out there but is ordered and  cosmos also has the connotation of a  kind of beauty of proportionality things  all being in their place if they are  dynamic rather than static functioning  the way that they ought to so the so  firm person the temperate person is in a  certain respect a beautiful person  insofar as their their soul is ordered  properly so there is this harmony going  on and and Plato will bring up the issue  of another closely related term self  mastery in Greek and Katya often opposed  to a crash'  Razia which which Socrates has a rather  unusual view on which we're not going to  go into a car SIA is when we know what  the good thing is but we don't do it or  we know what the bad thing is and we do  it  self-mastery and katya is when we know  what the good thing is and we make  ourselves do it even though we might be  tempted or impelled or all sorts of  other inducements may be brought on us  to do something different we we force  ourselves to do that and Plato says well  you know this is kind of a silly thing  he's joking around a